\section{One Command, Several Contracts}

The command that builds a C program is usually a driver, not just the program that translates C. In Section~1.4 of \emph{Linkers \& Loaders}, John R. Levine traces a two-file UNIX build: preprocess, compile, and assemble each file on its own, then link both objects with the system C library \cite[chapter~1]{levine2000linkers}. The important boundary is between independent translation and joint linking. The compiler need not see the definition of every function it calls; the object file must carry enough information for a later stage to finish the reference.

Treat the diagram as a description of work, not a required process tree. A current GCC driver may have its compiler executable do preprocessing and compilation in one invocation. Clang on many targets uses an integrated assembler rather than starting an external \texttt{as}. Some implementations can send machine-code bytes to an object writer without serializing assembly text. None of these choices eliminates the need to encode instructions, assign symbols, and represent unresolved references. Nor does an ordinary driver invocation decide which sources are stale: the build system commonly makes that decision, although Levine uses a broader sense of ``driver'' that includes build orchestration.

A description of the pipeline should identify its artifact boundaries. On a typical GNU/Linux ELF target, C preprocessing yields a translation unit in text form; compilation to assembly yields target-specific mnemonics and directives; assembly yields a relocatable object. A default final link through the C driver usually adds startup objects, runtime support, and the standard C library according to the toolchain's defaults. The result may use shared libraries and be position independent. Linking produces the file; launching it is another operation. Levine's separate account of loading explains why mapping the program and selecting any remaining run-time bindings are not jobs the driver performs by launching the executable.

\section{Request an Artifact, Not a Particular Subprocess}

Nora Sandler's \emph{Writing a C Compiler} exposes these boundaries in a teaching driver: it borrows GCC's preprocessor and assembler/linker and later adds the option to stop at an object file. With GCC, \texttt{-E} selects preprocessed output, \texttt{-S} stops at assembly, and \texttt{-c} stops at the object. The inputs also determine the start: a \texttt{.i} file is already preprocessed C, a lowercase \texttt{.s} file is ordinary assembly, and a \texttt{.o} file goes straight to the linking step. GCC conventionally preprocesses an uppercase \texttt{.S} assembly input. These conventions belong to the tool, not to ELF or to the C language.

The distinction matters when reproducing a failure. A compiler diagnostic about a missing declaration is not the same as a linker diagnostic about a missing definition. An assembly error can be isolated by retaining the compiler's assembly output and assembling it separately. If that works but the combined link fails, inspecting the actual object symbols and link inputs is more useful than adjusting the assembler's options.

