\section{Trace a Definition Across Two Translation Units}

In a scratch directory on GNU/Linux, use the following shell transcript with GCC and GNU Binutils. The caller expects a result of $5+7=12$; it returns a status of zero exactly when it gets that value.

\begin{verbatim}
printf '#define DELTA 7\n' > delta.h
printf '#include "delta.h"\n' > bias.c
printf 'int bias(int x) { return x + DELTA; }\n' >> bias.c
printf 'int bias(int);\n' > main.c
printf 'int main(void) { return bias(5) != 12; }\n' >> main.c

# The header substitution is visible before code generation.
gcc -E -P bias.c -o bias.i
cat bias.i
gcc -S bias.i -o bias.s
gcc -c bias.s -o bias.o
gcc -c main.c -o main.o

# A valid object can name a function it does not define.
nm main.o bias.o
gcc main.o bias.o -o check
./check
test "$?" -eq 0
\end{verbatim}

The preprocessed body contains \verb|return x + 7;|. The \texttt{nm} output shows \texttt{U bias} in \texttt{main.o} and \texttt{T bias} in \texttt{bias.o}, using GNU \texttt{nm}'s letters for an undefined symbol and a definition in text. For a negative test, run \verb|gcc main.o -o incomplete|: it fails to link even though \texttt{main.o} was successfully assembled. Alternatively, change the macro to eight and rebuild the provider: the link still succeeds, but the program returns status one since $5+8\ne12$. Successful binding finds a definition; it does not prove the definition implements the intended computation.

\Cref{fig:visual-279} shows the object files as the boundary where independent translation gives way to joint resolution.

